  "code": "1059",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<ul>\n<li data-start=\"1235\" data-end=\"1310\">\n<p data-start=\"120\" data-end=\"258\">The <strong data-start=\"124\" data-end=\"147\">Chromium Urine Spot</strong> test measures the amount of chromium excreted in a single (non-24-hour) urine sample. It is commonly used for:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"260\" data-end=\"584\">\n<li data-start=\"260\" data-end=\"350\">\n<p data-start=\"262\" data-end=\"350\"><strong data-start=\"262\" data-end=\"292\">Monitoring recent exposure</strong> to chromium, especially <strong data-start=\"317\" data-end=\"350\">occupational or environmental</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"351\" data-end=\"434\">\n<p data-start=\"353\" data-end=\"434\"><strong data-start=\"353\" data-end=\"396\">Assessing implant-related metal release</strong> (e.g., metal-on-metal hip prostheses)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"435\" data-end=\"492\">\n<p data-start=\"437\" data-end=\"492\"><strong data-start=\"437\" data-end=\"492\">Evaluating excessive dietary or supplemental intake</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"493\" data-end=\"584\">\n<p data-start=\"495\" data-end=\"584\"><strong data-start=\"495\" data-end=\"527\">Screening industrial workers</strong> (e.g., welders, electroplaters) for chromium VI exposure</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"586\" data-end=\"709\">Since chromium is excreted through the kidneys, urine levels reflect <strong data-start=\"655\" data-end=\"674\">recent exposure</strong> more accurately than serum levels.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Cause / Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Insight</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Occupational or environmental exposure</strong></td>\n<td>Inhalation or skin contact with chromium compounds (especially Cr⁶⁺) elevates levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Corrosion of chromium-containing implants</strong></td>\n<td>Such as metal-on-metal hip or knee replacements</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>High-dose chromium supplementation</strong></td>\n<td>Can increase urinary excretion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Acute dietary intake</strong></td>\n<td>Temporary rise may occur after meals high in chromium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Renal function</strong></td>\n<td>Though chromium is renally excreted, impaired function may reduce excretion slightly</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
